WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
Speaker 1: another legal problem for Donald&nbsp;
Trump that I want to briefly talk about and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:10.440
then we're going to move on to other things. A&nbsp;
federal judge has found that Donald Trump lied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:16.680
under oath about voter fraud in Georgia while&nbsp;
trying to overturn the 2020 election results.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:23.100
Now we're switching gears. Before we were talking&nbsp;
about the federal investigation that is connected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:29.100
to the search warrant at Mar a Lago. That's&nbsp;
the last story. This is a different situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:36.780
now. Business Insider summarizes the statement&nbsp;
by the judge. A federal judge Wednesday said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:44.280
Trump lied under oath about voter fraud claims in&nbsp;
the 2020 election. Not a big shock. Trump, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:51.840
knew that the specific numbers of voter fraud were&nbsp;
wrong, but touted them both in court and to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:58.080
public. This is per Judge David Carter. Carter&nbsp;
made the determination in a ruling ordering a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:05.640
Republican lawyer to turn over his communications&nbsp;
to the January 6th committee. This goes back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:12.600
to December 4th of 2020. And I know many of you&nbsp;
remember this. Many of you don't. Many of you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:18.780
chosen to forget it. And I understand I understand&nbsp;
why. On December 4th, 2020, Trump's legal team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:25.500
filed a lawsuit in Georgia State Court alleging&nbsp;
that Fulton County miscounted thousands of votes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:30.240
They also contested the state courts proceedings&nbsp;
in federal court. This was one of dozens of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:35.700
complaints that Trump and lawyers filed in various&nbsp;
courts. Later that month, Carter wrote in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:40.920
ruling Eastman This is Trump's lawyer. Eastman&nbsp;
said in an email that although Trump had, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:47.220
signed a verification for the state court filing&nbsp;
on December one, he had since been made aware that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:52.740
some of the allegations and evidence proffered&nbsp;
by the experts has been inaccurate. For him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:58.740
sign a new certification with that knowledge and&nbsp;
incorporation by reference would not be accurate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:05.160
he added. The emails show that Trump knew that&nbsp;
the specific numbers of voter fraud were wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:12.360
but continued to tout those numbers both in court&nbsp;
and to the public. This is also not the first time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:18.960
that this has been suggested. So it's abundantly&nbsp;
clear that in every single one of these cases, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:25.920
have claims that are made that normal people who&nbsp;
look at data and evidence doubt. We ask ourselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:32.460
are they making these claims out of ignorance&nbsp;
or are they making these claims knowing they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:36.840
are false claims in order to deceive others? And&nbsp;
we don't necessarily know right away. But then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:41.820
slowly but surely, we come to learn that these&nbsp;
claims are made knowing that they are lying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:48.720
in the Mar a Lago DOJ situation. Did Trump&nbsp;
believe that they really had given back all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:54.360
the documents? I don't know. Maybe we later find&nbsp;
out. Trump knew they had not given back all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:59.040
documents because there is a report that he had&nbsp;
an employee move documents at Mar a Lago. Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:03.720
okay. So he was lying. He was lying deliberately&nbsp;
to deceive. Arguably an obstructive act.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:10.020
Same thing here. Okay. At the beginning of&nbsp;
December, Trump signs off on We believe these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:15.180
are the numbers of fraudulent, phony, invalid&nbsp;
votes from Georgia. Maybe Trump believed it at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:21.840
that point in time. We then get testimony and&nbsp;
statements under oath that actually later on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:27.660
when Trump continued to sign off on this stuff,&nbsp;
Trump had been advised that what he was claiming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:35.340
and signing off on was not actually the truth.&nbsp;
Unfortunately, Georgia State Law says that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:46.140
punishment here is up to one year in jail and up&nbsp;
to a $1,000 fine or both. This is 2020 Georgia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:53.280
Code. Title 16 Crimes and Offenses. Chapter ten&nbsp;
Offenses Against Public Administration. Article&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.280 --> 00:04:00.840
four Perjury and Related Offenses. And it says,&nbsp;
Punished by a fine of not more than $1,000 or by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:08.340
imprisonment, not less than one, not more than&nbsp;
ten years or both. But the particulars of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:16.620
likely point to no more than one year in prison,&nbsp;
one year in jail, rather. You know, there is this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:21.960
question is whether as to whether the American&nbsp;
justice system should really assess fines as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:26.400
function of income, because one of the realities&nbsp;
that we have in the justice system in the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:33.420
States with such drastically different financial&nbsp;
circumstances for people are that the same $5,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:44.040
fine can be a completely unpayable bankruptcy&nbsp;
inducing fine for many in society, and then such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:50.640
an insignificant fine for others in society&nbsp;
that it does nothing whatsoever to actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:55.620
punish a bad act. And so there are countries that&nbsp;
do this with speeding tickets, for example. We've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:59.400
talked about it before. I believe Norway might&nbsp;
be one of those countries, but I don't remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:04.440
completely offhand. But there is a real question&nbsp;
as to whether fines should be assessed based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:09.000
ability to pay with some upper and lower&nbsp;
limit. Certainly, I think that reasonable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:15.180
people could agree to that. So not a particularly&nbsp;
auspicious day yesterday for Donald Trump from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:19.440
legal standpoint, but when we continue to ask&nbsp;
the question, well, but then so what? What's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:25.020
coming? Is anything coming? I think we all are&nbsp;
in the position of saying we don't really know.

